<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Cook</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1779">1779</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Captain Cook</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Cook was a British naval officer who led three voyages of exploration in the Pacific between 1768 and 1779. He charted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, visited Tahiti and the Society Islands, named New Caledonia in 1774 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> in 1778. He was killed in a clash at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kealakekua Bay</a> on Hawaii Island in February 1779.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-cook.jpg" alt="James Cook" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathaniel Dance-Holland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Captainjamescookportrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Samuel Wallis reached Tahiti in HMS Dolphin in 1767, followed by Louis-Antoine de Bougainville in 1768 and <b>James Cook</b> in 1769, and European captains came to treat Tu, chief of Pare and Arue near Matavai Bay, as the island’s king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…and between the two confederations, and some writers have linked the toppling of the moai to such conflicts: when <b>James Cook</b> visited in 1774 some statues had already fallen, and by the middle of the nineteenth century almost all the statues on the platforms had been pulled down.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1777 <b>James Cook</b> watched the ʻinasi first-fruits ceremony at Muʻa and wrote that its occasion was the Tuʻi Tonga Paulaho and his son eating together, a thing never done before.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>James Cook</b>’s crew first wrote it, in 1778, they spelled it Owhyhee, catching the little word ʻo that comes before a proper name: ʻO Hawaiʻi, “[This] is Hawaiʻi”.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Māori language, te reo Māori, belongs to the Eastern Polynesian languages, close to Cook Islands Māori, Tuamotuan and Tahitian; it stayed so close that the Tahitian navigator Tupaia could interpret for <b>James Cook</b>’s crew in 1769.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maori">Māori · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote><b>James Cook</b> reached the islands in 1778 and was killed at Kealakekua Bay in 1779 after trying to take Kalaniʻōpuʻu, ruler of Hawaiʻi Island, hostage.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Marton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kealakekua Bay</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.54,-1.21&amp;year=1761" aria-label="Marton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4523 296 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Cook"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4973 566h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4973 566h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.54,-1.21&amp;year=1761">What was here in 1761? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I, who had ambition not only to go farther than any one had been before, but as far as it was possible for man to go, was not sorry at meeting with this interruption…&#8221;</p><footer>A Voyage Towards the South Pole, on meeting the ice, January 1774 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15777/pg15777-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;From what I have said of the Natives of New Holland they may appear to some to be the most wretched People upon Earth; but in reality they are far more happier than we Europeans…&#8221;</p><footer>Journal of the first voyage, August 1770 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8106/pg8106-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;They live in a Tranquility which is not disturbed by the Inequality of Condition. The earth and Sea of their own accord furnishes them with all things necessary for Life.&#8221;</p><footer>Journal of the first voyage, August 1770, on the people of New Holland &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8106/pg8106-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
